WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.120
It's been two weeks since you won

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.820
what has this period been like?

00:00:13.865 --> 00:00:16.645
It's been such a joy! But

00:00:16.645 --> 00:00:18.325
I've been sick.

00:00:18.325 --> 00:00:20.300
And you might hear it in my voice.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.460
I came home after a lot of rehearsals.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.320
The pressure of having the lowest odds.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.200
And

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.280
also the feeling of when I'd hit the last note ...

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.685
I felt that, «I may have won now».

00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:35.585
Because it really felt awesome!

00:00:36.085 --> 00:00:39.275
But wen that later becomes reality, and you get

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.940
eight 12's from the international jury

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:44.980
and Sweden's vote ...

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.460
That's quite a lot to process.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:49.760
Yes!

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:51.280
Yes!

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.500
And then your body just goes ...

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.340
Yes, a little like that.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.720
The shop is closed?

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.460
The day after, I had to get up at eight.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.000
After the after party.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.200
Because we're filming a show that's called

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.340
«The day after», which is about ...

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.240
Yeah, right!

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.335
Which is about what it feels like the day after.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:19.285
And after that you get home to a two-year-old,

00:01:19.285 --> 00:01:22.380
a four-year-old and a spouse.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.020
«DAAAAD!»

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.560
And my body then said «wait a minute now».

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:32.220
And I told my spouse and my children:

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.260
«I'm just going to rest for a little while.»

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.660
24 hours later

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.440
I wake up

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.800
and realise that I've been sleeping for 24 hours!

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.480
You slept for 24 hours?!

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.660
Yes. I was completely finished!

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.240
You spoke about your background. And your

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.720
childhood was here in London, actually.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.160
You grew up here and lived here until you were six.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.520
Correct.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.260
If you were to do a flashback to

00:02:03.535 --> 00:02:06.495
the schoolyard here in London

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.000
compared to the schoolyard in Växjö, where you moved.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:10.860
Yes.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.975
What is the difference?

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.460
The biggest difference is that you have the entire world in London.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.920
When it comes to culture, colour and background.

00:02:23.035 --> 00:02:24.145
And

00:02:24.675 --> 00:02:27.865
I never understood that I was different.

00:02:27.865 --> 00:02:31.065
Because everyone else was different in the schoolyard.

00:02:31.065 --> 00:02:34.260
Everyone had different backgrounds, different clothes,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.480
different colours.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:42.140
And there was a culture shock when we came to Växjö.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:43.660
Which is in the South of Sweden.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.600
Yes, exactly, Småland.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.860
Växjö has been fantastic in every way

00:02:49.865 --> 00:02:51.600
and I have Växjö to thank in a lot of ways.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:55.040
But going to school from grade one to six was ...

00:02:55.165 --> 00:02:58.135
It was pretty tough for me, because I was

00:02:58.135 --> 00:03:00.885
the only one who had dark skin

00:03:00.885 --> 00:03:04.205
in that particular schoolyard, in contradiction to London.

00:03:04.205 --> 00:03:05.720
And those were tough years.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.060
And that was partly about the n-word.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.260
When I heard that for the first time

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.080
I didn't understand what it was.

00:03:13.205 --> 00:03:16.125
And I run home to my parents

00:03:16.125 --> 00:03:19.185
up the hill, open the door

00:03:19.425 --> 00:03:20.965
and I remember it like it was yesterday.

00:03:20.965 --> 00:03:24.380
My parents are sitting at the kitchen table, eating together.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.240
And my name is Jompa at home, not John, Jompa.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:32.480
And I come in and say:

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.460
«Mom and dad, what does 'fucking negro' mean?»

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.320
And their glance in that second

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.440
said more than a thousand words.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:43.920
Because they knew that

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.380
«Okay. You're going to go through this kind of period now.»

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.480
And having kids myself

00:03:51.595 --> 00:03:54.855
I dread that period, if it will happen to my children.

00:03:54.855 --> 00:03:56.475
But it was ...

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:01.520
It was such a powerful memory, because I had never experienced

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:04.740
that it was negative being different.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.120
Because that little six year old boy

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:10.635
just wanted to play football

00:04:10.635 --> 00:04:12.545
and be just like everyone else.

00:04:12.545 --> 00:04:14.060
As loved as everyone else.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.860
Is there any of them ...

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:22.400
Is there any of them you remember that you can't let go of?

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:23.640
Of the bullies?

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.380
No, I usually say: «Kill'em with a smile».

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.200
And ...

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.240
And

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.360
in one way it's like «look at me now, I was right»!

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:41.380
But ...

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:46.800
But what became the turning point, when did it turn around, when did it end?

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.680
I called it «the race that changed my life».

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.020
We had a sports day at school.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:57.700
And I had dressed up so nicely, I had tights on, and a tank top!

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:03.440
And I had glasses with a granny chain so they wouldn't fall down!

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.780
I was ready for war!

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:10.400
And the people I was running against, they were the ones who had been the meanest.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.760
And in some way

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.395
I was running for my life, but also as a relief

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.600
because when I crossed the finish line

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.120
I was so far ahead.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.860
I won.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.880
And for the first time, I was best at something.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.900
I had beat them at something.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.580
Everything turned then, because I had gained my identity through sports.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:35.180
You got Swedish Championship medals in both in the junior and youth championships.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:36.520
Yes, correct.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.000
What would you say was your biggest accomplishment?

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.600
Wow. That probably is that I quit!

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.120
But BEFORE that!

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:53.560
I would probably say my personal record of 10.84.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.120
It was one of those runs where everything felt right.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.495
You were 23 when you

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:03.440
chose music instead, you had been doing music all along.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:10.180
And then you went to Stockholm, and you wanted to pursue music.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.540
And a lot of people said no.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:16.980
Yes. I would say I had probably 50-60 CDs

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.980
made. They were hand written.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:23.440
And with the last CD

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:25.920
I knocked on Jörgen Elofsson's door.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:27.380
At Lidingö.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:28.920
Who is a very well-known songwriter.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.540
Yes, it's one of the biggest we have.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.380
And I knocked the door, drew a big breath, I had no money in my pocket

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:39.180
because I had spent everything on a taxi, one had to take taxi to be fancy!

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.720
And I knocked on the door

00:06:42.875 --> 00:06:45.675
and I think that he saw someplace in my eyes that

00:06:45.675 --> 00:06:48.795
if this guy has traveled out here and done this

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:52.260
he must want this quite a lot.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.800
So he said: «Well, young boy, you have one minute

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:00.140
from now». He has a Skåne dialect.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:02.420
Sorry, Jörgen.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.980
And then I had to try to find my coolness and insert the CD in the player.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:13.180
And I manage to in the end and

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.060
Jörgen presses play.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.680
And he stops the CD after a while

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:21.500
and he literally says:

00:07:23.595 --> 00:07:26.265
«Yeah. Have you written this?»

00:07:26.795 --> 00:07:29.015
«Yes.» I'm sitting in a chair.

00:07:29.655 --> 00:07:32.025
«Okay.» And he starts playing it again.

00:07:32.025 --> 00:07:33.500
And he presses pause.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:37.100
«It's like a nicely wrapped poo!»

00:07:39.815 --> 00:07:40.815
And

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.320
the panic at this point is very overwhelming.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:47.940
But afterwords he asks: «Who is singing?»

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.140
«Who is singing?» in Swedish.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:54.195
And I take a deep breath and say:

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:55.940
«I'm the one who is singing.»

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:58.640
And he says: «Be here at 11 tomorrow.»

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:00.220
I have a job for you.

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:04.500
So I became his personal demo singer.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.435
And started recording his hits,

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:09.500
his demos, all around Europe,

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:11.020
and in Stockholm.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:15.740
The first highlight in your music career

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:19.200
after having knocked on doors and fought a long time

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.960
was that you together with Jörgen Elofsson

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:27.480
got to write a song

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:30.140
ordered by prince Daniel.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:31.020
Yes.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:33.260
For the wedding of Victoria and Daniel.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:34.400
Yes.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:38.000
What was that like for your parents?

00:08:38.165 --> 00:08:40.655
We had a confidentiality agreement.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:44.300
Because I did travel to Växjö to my parents

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:46.320
and my brother.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:51.180
And we were sitting in the living room, and it's like a scene from a TV show.

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:57.740
We're sitting there watching these fantastic people who are getting married, with everything that's happening.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:59.660
And then it's time for the wedding.

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:05.520
«I'm standing by an angel ...»

00:09:06.705 --> 00:09:09.825
And the text appears with

00:09:09.825 --> 00:09:11.280
our names.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.100
Right, because it says the title of the song!

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:15.620
Yes! «Written by!»

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:17.480
And my mom goes:

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:31.560
And my dad tries to play it cool, but even he is like:

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.440
In some kind of shock mode.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.200
So they completely lost it.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.420
They couldn't believe it was true!

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:45.780
But that's what happened.

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:49.040
And then you've also written the British contribution

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:51.980
Yes!

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:56.500
Thank you.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.980
That's a slightly different song.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.720
Can you give us a little taste of how it goes?

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.080
Yes, if you behave nicely!

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:11.360
I'm very nice!

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:12.300
But it's pretty simple.

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:40.460
Sorry.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:43.480
You have ...

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.060
You have made it very hard for yourself.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:48.580
Thank you for that!

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:50.820
You're going to compete against yourself!

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:51.740
Yes.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:52.520
In Tel Aviv.

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:53.340
Yes.

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:55.420
Who is going to win?

00:10:57.860 --> 00:10:59.160
Sweden will win!

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.540
John, thank you for being here tonight!

